# Git write operations — user only

The repository owner is the **only** person who runs git commands that change history, remotes, or merge state.

## Agents must NOT

- Run `git commit`, `git merge`, `git rebase`, `git cherry-pick`, `git am`, or any command that creates, rewrites, or concludes history (including `git merge --continue`, `git rebase --continue`, `git cherry-pick --continue`)
- Start or leave an in-progress merge/rebase/cherry-pick (e.g. `git merge`, `git merge --no-commit`, `git rebase`, `git cherry-pick`) — even when the user asks to bring in another branch or repo
- Run `git push`, `git push --force`, or publish branches/tags to remotes
- Run `git reset` (any mode), `git checkout`/`git restore` to discard the user’s work, or other destructive history/working-tree git operations unless the user explicitly requests that exact command in the current message
- Run `git add` / stage unless the user explicitly asked to stage in the current message

## Syncing from another branch or repo

When the user wants changes from another branch or checkout (e.g. upstream `develop` in another path):

- **Do:** compare trees with read-only git (`git diff`, `git show`, `git log`) or by reading files on disk; **edit or copy file contents** in the working tree so the result matches what they asked for; resolve conflicts in file text, not with `git merge`
- **Do not:** use `git merge`, `git cherry-pick`, or any workflow that sets MERGE_HEAD / leaves “merging” state — the user performs merges and commits themselves

## Agents MAY

- Edit files in the working tree (unstaged by default)
- Run read-only git commands (`git status`, `git diff`, `git log`, `git show`, etc.)
- Prepare suggested commit messages, merge commands, or PR text for the user to run manually
- Explain what the user should run to commit, merge, push, or rebase themselves

## When the user wants a commit, merge, push, or rebase

Wait for clear wording such as “commit this”, “merge develop”, “push to origin”, or “rebase onto …”. If intent is ambiguous, ask. **Never** run those commands on the user’s behalf unless they explicitly ask for that exact git operation in the current message (this repo’s default is: user only).
